A||||3||Roy||Removal Of The Transaxle||||8||
Z||000000||Roy||12-12-1999||07:38 PM||||Is it possible to remove the 4spd manual transaxle from <BR>84 se. I don't want to have to drop the engine too... I <BR>was just wondering if anyone knows a way to take<BR>it off without remove the engine... I need to replace<BR>the clutch, pessure plate, throw out bearing...ect. <P>Roy<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Sage||12-12-1999||09:13 PM||||Have not done it this way myself, but you can supposedly, remove the axles, trans mounts and anything else in the way and leave the whole mess in there.  I have a mechanic friend who says he has done this, and there is enough room to pull the transaxle back(toward the drivers side)so you can get to the preasure plate bolts and change the whole assembly without removing the tranny or engine.  I prefer to drop the whole cradle.  The Haynes manual covers removing just the transaxle, but it seems like more work than removing the cradle to me!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||12-13-1999||11:31 AM||||Never replaced a clutch that way but I understand there isn't much room to work, but it can be done.<BR>I did pull a 5speed out of an 86 by pulling the rear cradle bolts.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Did want to caution you here. After you unhook the tranny the only thing holding the motor in is the front motor mount (the one on the right side of the car) so you will need to support the motor in some manner. Either put a jack under your oil pan or a 2X6 across your shock towers and chain to it.<BR>GOOD LUCK.||||reg||6
Z||000003||David||12-13-1999||05:30 PM||||Roy<P>I've changed the clutch on my '86 4cyl, 5sp twice by pulling the engine (only) out the top.  If I had it to do again, I would do it the same way.  ||||reg||1
